    # h21=(2,1)-(2,2), h22=(2,3)-(2,4)
    # h31=(3,1)-(3,2), h32=(3,3)-(3,4)
    # h41=(4,1)-(4,2), h42=(4,3)-(4,4)
    # Flips:
    # f11: columns 1,2; rows 1,2 (v11, v12)
    # f21: columns 1,2; rows 2,3 (v21, v22)
    # f31: columns 1,2; rows 3,4 (v31, v32)
    # f12: columns 3,4; rows 1,2 (v13, v14)
    # f22: columns 3,4; rows 2,3 (v23, v24)
    # f32: columns 3,4; rows 3,4 (v33, v34)
    # These are 6 flips.
    # Each flip f_ij must be broken by marking either v_i_2j-1 or v_i_2j.
    # Let v_ij be the vertical domino at row i, col j.
    # v_i_2j-1 = {(i, 2j-1), (i+1, 2j-1)}
    # v_i_2j = {(i, 2j), (i+1, 2j)}
    # Total 12 vertical dominoes.
    # Flip f_i_c (i=1,2,3; c=1,2) is {v_i,2c-1, v_i,2c}.
    # We must pick one domino from each of the 6 sets:
    # {v_1,1, v_1,2}, {v_2,1, v_2,2}, {v_3,1, v_3,2},
    # {v_1,3, v_1,4}, {v_2,3, v_2,4}, {v_3,3, v_3,4}
    # Wait, these 6 sets are disjoint!
    # Let's check:
    # v_1,1 = {(1,1), (2,1)}
    # v_1,2 = {(1,2), (2,2)}
    # v_2,1 = {(2,1), (3,1)}
    # v_2,2 = {(2,2), (3,2)}
    # v_3,1 = {(3,1), (4,1)}
    # v_3,2 = {(3,2), (4,2)}
    # v_1,3 = {(1,3), (2,3)}
    # v_1,4 = {(1,4), (2,4)}
    # v_2,3 = {(2,3), (3,3)}
    # v_2,4 = {(2,4), (3,4)}
    # v_3,3 = {(3,3), (4,3)}
    # v_3,4 = {(3,4), (4,4)}
    # Are they disjoint?
    # v_1,1 and v_2,1 share (2,1).
    # v_1,2 and v_2,2 share (2,2).
    # v_2,1 and v_3,1 share (3,1).
    # v_2,2 and v_3,2 share (3,2).
    # Similarly for the other columns.
    # So for column 1,2, we have 3 flip positions.
    # The dominoes are:
    # f11: {v_1,1, v_1,2}
    # f21: {v_2,1, v_2,2}
    # f31: {v_3,1, v_3,2}
    # We want to pick one from each set.
